--- field source.rsi.slot
type: uint

Which registry source slot supplied the data, by index. Sources are the
userspace backends that serve registry content to the kernel, so this names
the backend rather than anything about the caller.

--- field source.rsi.hive
type: str

The hive the offending source data belonged to, where the failure could be
attributed to one. Absent when the data was malformed before a hive could
be determined.

--- field request.id
type: uint

The identifier of the source request this record concerns, for joining a
record to the exchange that produced it. A pure correlation key.

--- field request.op-code
type: uint

The source-protocol operation code of the request this record concerns.
Identifies which kind of exchange returned unusable data.

--- field request.op-code-expected
type: uint

The source-protocol operation code the kernel expected a response to carry,
recorded where the source answered with a different one. Read it beside
`request.op-code`, which holds the code that actually arrived; the
disagreement between the two is the finding.

A response code is the request code with the high bit `0x8000` set, so an
expected code of `0x8021` answers a request of `0x0021` (`RSI_SET_VALUE`).

--- field request.status
type: str.enum
values: ok | not-found | already-exists | storage-error | not-empty | too-large | txn-busy | invalid | cas-failed | txn-not-supported
closed: false

The RSI status code a registry source returned for a request, named from
the `RSI_*` status constants with the prefix removed. The kernel maps each
status to an errno before the caller sees it, and the errno loses the
source's own account: `storage-error` and a response the kernel could not
use at all both reach the caller as `EIO`, and only this field says which
it was.

A status code outside this list is never carried here. It is rejected as
malformed source data and recorded on `lcs.source.response.rejected` with
the reason `unknown-rsi-status-code`.

--- field request.deferred-effect
type: str.enum
values: restore-commit | key-mutation
closed: false

The kind of kernel-side work attached to a source request, to be applied
when the source's reply arrives rather than when the caller returns.
`restore-commit` completes a registry restore; `key-mutation` publishes
the effects of a key write, such as watch notifications and generation
updates. Absent when the request carries no deferred work.

Deferred work is what makes a late reply consequential. When the caller
has already given up (`request.waiter-detached`), the effect is still
applied, so a change the caller was told had failed can land afterwards.

--- field request.waited
type: bool

Whether the kernel blocked waiting for the source's reply to a request.
False for a request dispatched without a waiter, whose outcome no caller
will see directly.

--- field request.timed-out
type: bool

Whether a request's wait for the source's reply ran out before the reply
arrived. The deadline is the configured request timeout. A timed-out
request is not cancelled at the source: its reply may still arrive, and
any deferred effect attached to it may still be applied.

--- field request.waiter-detached
type: bool

Whether the caller that issued a request had stopped waiting by the time
the source's reply arrived. True means the operation had already reported
failure to userspace, typically a timeout, so any effect the reply carries
lands after the caller was told it did not. This is the entry condition for
the kernel's late-effect replay.

--- field source.rsi.generation
type: uint

The restart generation of a registry source slot: how many times the slot
has been resumed by a re-registering source after going down. Zero for a
source that has never been restarted. A count that climbs quickly is a
source crash-looping.

--- field source.rsi.generation-validated
type: uint

The source restart generation that a cached key handle was last validated
against. A handle whose validated generation is behind
`source.rsi.generation` has outlived a source restart and must be
revalidated before use, because the key it names may no longer exist.

--- field source.rsi.in-flight
type: uint

The number of requests dispatched to a registry source and still awaiting
a reply, at the moment of the record. Bounded by the configured concurrency
ceiling per source, so a value at that ceiling means new requests are
queuing behind slow ones.

--- field source.rsi.queued
type: uint

The number of requests queued for a registry source and not yet delivered
to it, at the moment of the record. Distinct from `source.rsi.in-flight`:
a queued request has not reached the source at all, so it is discarded
rather than failed if the source goes down.

--- field mutation.sequence
type: uint

The sequence number LCS stamped on a registry mutation. Allocated from the
kernel's global monotonic counter, so sequences order writes across every
hive and source, and the higher sequence wins when two writes land in
layers of equal precedence.

The counter resumes at boot from one past the highest sequence any
registered source reports. A record's sequence is therefore comparable
with any other from the same registry, not only those from the same boot.

--- field mutation.sequence-expected
type: uint

The sequence number a conditional write asserted as the value's current
one, the precondition of a compare-and-swap. The write proceeds only if
the value still carries this sequence, and fails with `cas-failed` from the
source otherwise. Absent on an unconditional write.

--- field mutation.sequence-watermark
type: uint

The next sequence number the kernel would allocate, at the moment of the
record. Every sequence legitimately stored in the registry is below it, so
a source reporting a sequence at or above the watermark is claiming a
write the kernel never allocated, which is the future-sequence check that
`lcs.source.response.rejected` records.

--- field object.hive.name
type: str

The name of the registry hive an operation acted on, such as `Machine` or
`Users`. A hive is the unit a registry source registers to serve, so this
names which source's namespace the operation reached.

--- field object.layer.name
type: str

The name of the registry layer an operation acted on, as written when the
layer was created. Layer names are compared without regard to case, so
two records whose names differ only in case name the same layer. The base
layer is named `base`.

--- field object.layer.base
type: bool

Whether an operation targeted the base layer, the bottom of every key's
resolution order. The base layer always exists at precedence 0 and cannot
be disabled.

--- field object.layer.enabled
type: bool

Whether a registry layer is enabled, after the change the record
describes. A disabled layer takes no part in resolution: its values exist
but no reader sees them.

--- field object.layer.enabled-previous
type: bool

Whether a registry layer was enabled before the change the record
describes. Read with `object.layer.enabled`: a record where the two differ
is a layer being switched on or off.

--- field object.layer.precedence
type: uint

A registry layer's precedence, after the change the record describes.
Where two layers define the same value, the one with the higher
precedence wins, and the higher sequence breaks a tie. This number
therefore decides which value the whole system sees.

--- field object.layer.precedence-previous
type: uint

A registry layer's precedence before the change the record describes.
Read with `object.layer.precedence`: raising a layer above another
silently changes which of their values every reader resolves.

--- field object.layer.resolution-changed
type: bool

Whether a change to a layer altered what any reader of the registry
actually resolves, as opposed to changing a layer whose values were
already outranked. LCS computes this and discards it today, so it is not
yet emitted on any record.

--- field object.watch.filter
type: uint.flags
values: 0x1 REG_NOTIFY_VALUE | 0x2 REG_NOTIFY_SUBKEY | 0x4 REG_NOTIFY_SD

The categories of change a process asked a registry watch to report: value
changes, subkey creation and deletion, and security descriptor changes. A
watcher is told only of changes in the categories it set.

--- field object.watch.subtree
type: bool

Whether a registry watch covers the whole subtree beneath its key rather
than the key alone. How deep a subtree watch reaches is
`object.watch.depth-limit`, and that limit is unbounded by default.

--- field object.watch.depth-limit
type: uint

The maximum depth beneath its key at which a subtree watch reports
changes. 0 means unbounded, and 0 is the default. The limit is the
machine-wide `MaxSubtreeWatchDepth` setting rather than anything the
watcher chose, so changes deeper than it are suppressed for every subtree
watch at once.

--- field object.watch.event
type: str.enum
values: value-set | value-deleted | subkey-created | subkey-deleted | sd-changed | key-deleted | overflow
closed: false

The type of a registry watch event, named from its `REG_WATCH_*` constant
with the prefix removed. `overflow` replaces individual events when they
could not all be delivered, telling the watcher only that it missed
something and must re-read the key.

An `overflow` does not always mean a full queue. It is also queued when a
single event could not be encoded, so a watcher seeing one cannot tell
which happened.

--- field object.watch.effects
type: uint.flags
values: 0x1 layer-delete

The internal effects that a registry commit triggered through the
kernel's own configuration watches. `layer-delete` means the watch
dispatch for the commit already handled a layer deletion, so the commit
skips its own separate layer-delete step. Absent when the commit
triggered none.

--- group caller
include: subject.token.sid
include: subject.token.integrity
include: subject.token.id
include: subject.token.auth-id
include: subject.token.type
include: subject.token.impersonation

The identity LCS records on an audited operation. A deliberate subset of
`subject` — the token's durable GUIDs ride in the event header, and the
group SIDs and PIP state that KACS records are absent here.

**That absence is a gap rather than a design.** Access to a registry key is
granted through a group as often as to a user SID directly, so an LCS
record cannot answer "which membership let this through" where the
equivalent KACS record can. The two subsystems reached the bound on what to
serialise independently and landed in different places.

--- event lcs.audit.key.opened
tier: essential
gating: a matching SACL audit ACE on the key
cardinality: once per matching open

The record that a registry key was opened and what access the open
received. SACL evaluation follows the KACS AccessCheck algorithm, with the
SACL evaluated alongside the DACL rather than separately.

**A request of `MAXIMUM_ALLOWED` alone records nothing.** It maps to a
desired mask of zero, and the SACL walk tests each audit ACE's mask against
that mask, so no ACE matches. The one request shape that asks for
everything is the one shape that produces no record — a real hole for
anyone auditing key opens, and the same defect KACS's own SACL walk was
changed to close.

Emission failure is handled in two halves. If a valid payload cannot be
**constructed**, the open fails with `EIO` and no key descriptor is
published — the audit is a precondition of the access. If a valid payload
cannot be **retained** by KMES, the decision and the descriptor stand, and
the loss is accounted as a gap in the stream rather than as a failed open.

include: caller
field: object.kind              required
  Always `key`. Names the table the access masks decode against.
field: object.key.guid          required
field: access.requested         required
  After registry generic mapping, with `MAXIMUM_ALLOWED` re-added when the
  caller asked for it.
field: access.granted           required
  Forced to zero on a denial, and enforced rather than merely intended — a
  denied record carrying a non-zero granted mask is rejected as malformed.
field: outcome.success          required
  Whether the open was allowed. Replaces the former `decision` string,
  which carried the same two states as words.
field: trigger.sacl-match       required

--- event lcs.audit.value.set
tier: essential
gating: KEY_SET_VALUE overlaps the alarm mask cached on the key handle
cardinality: once per write through a handle whose mask covers it

The record that a registry value was written through a key handle, or that
an attempt to write one failed. The key's `SYSTEM_ALARM` ACEs configure it:
the ACEs that match the caller when the key is opened give the handle a
continuous-audit mask, and every later write whose right overlaps the mask
is recorded, as `kacs.audit.handle.used` does for files.

**The mask is fixed when the key is opened.** Removing the alarm ACE does
not silence a handle already open, so the write that removes it is still
recorded; equally, adding one does not reach handles opened before.

**A transacted write is recorded when it is staged, not when it lands.**
`outcome.success` then means the source accepted it into the transaction;
whether the transaction took effect is the matching
`lcs.audit.transaction.committed`, joined on `transaction.id`.

**A timed-out write may have been applied later.** The source is not told
to cancel, and when its reply arrives the kernel still applies the write's
effects, with nothing recording who made it. Such a record says
`outcome.success` false with `request.timed-out` true: read it as "may
have been applied".

The data itself is never recorded, only its type, length and SHA-256
digest, with the same three for the value it replaced.

include: caller
field: object.kind              required
  Always `key`.
field: object.key.guid          required
field: object.key.path          required
field: object.key.layer.name    optional
  The layer written, `base` when the caller named none. Absent only when
  the request failed before its layer was read.
field: object.key.value.name    optional
  Absent only when the request failed before its name was read.
field: object.key.value.type    optional
  The type written. Present once the data was read from the caller.
field: object.key.value.length  optional
  Present with `object.key.value.type`.
field: object.key.value.digest  optional
  Present with `object.key.value.type`.
field: object.key.value.type-previous    optional
  The effective value before the write. Only on a write outside a
  transaction that found one: a transacted write never reads it.
field: object.key.value.length-previous  optional
  Present with `object.key.value.type-previous`.
field: object.key.value.digest-previous  optional
  Present with `object.key.value.type-previous`.
field: access.requested         required
  Always `KEY_SET_VALUE`.
field: access.granted           required
  What the handle was opened with.
field: access.matched           required
field: access.audit-mask        required
field: mutation.sequence        optional
  The sequence stamped on the write. Absent only when the request failed
  before one was allocated.
field: mutation.sequence-expected  optional
  Present on a compare-and-swap write.
field: transaction.id           optional
  Present when the write was staged in a transaction.
field: request.timed-out        when outcome.success == false
  True when the source did not answer in time, so the write may have
  been applied after all.
field: outcome.success          required
field: outcome.errno            when outcome.success == false

--- event lcs.audit.value.deleted
tier: essential
gating: KEY_SET_VALUE overlaps the alarm mask cached on the key handle
cardinality: once per delete through a handle whose mask covers it

The record that a registry value was deleted through a key handle, or that
an attempt to delete one failed. Gated, staged and fixed at open exactly as
`lcs.audit.value.set` is.

What was removed is recorded by type, length and SHA-256 digest: the
effective value before the delete, read in the transaction's own view when
the delete is transacted.

include: caller
field: object.kind              required
  Always `key`.
field: object.key.guid          required
field: object.key.path          required
field: object.key.layer.name    optional
  The layer whose entry was deleted. Absent only when the request failed
  before its layer was read.
field: object.key.value.name    optional
  Absent only when the request failed before its name was read.
field: object.key.value.type-previous    optional
  Absent when no value was found to delete.
field: object.key.value.length-previous  optional
  Present with `object.key.value.type-previous`.
field: object.key.value.digest-previous  optional
  Present with `object.key.value.type-previous`.
field: access.requested         required
  Always `KEY_SET_VALUE`.
field: access.granted           required
field: access.matched           required
field: access.audit-mask        required
field: transaction.id           optional
field: request.timed-out        when outcome.success == false
field: outcome.success          required
field: outcome.errno            when outcome.success == false

--- event lcs.audit.key.tombstoned
tier: essential
gating: KEY_SET_VALUE overlaps the alarm mask cached on the key handle
cardinality: once per blanket tombstone change through a handle whose mask covers it

The record that a key's blanket tombstone was set or cleared in a layer, or
that an attempt failed. A blanket tombstone hides every value lower layers
hold for the key at once, so setting one is a bulk value change and is
gated on `KEY_SET_VALUE`, as value writes are.

include: caller
field: object.kind              required
  Always `key`.
field: object.key.guid          required
field: object.key.path          required
field: object.key.layer.name    optional
  The layer the blanket belongs to.
field: operation.name           optional
  values: set | clear
  closed: true
  Whether the blanket was set or cleared. Absent only when the request
  failed before it was read.
field: access.requested         required
  Always `KEY_SET_VALUE`.
field: access.granted           required
field: access.matched           required
field: access.audit-mask        required
field: mutation.sequence        optional
  Present when a blanket was set: clearing one stamps no sequence.
field: transaction.id           optional
field: request.timed-out        when outcome.success == false
field: outcome.success          required
field: outcome.errno            when outcome.success == false

--- event lcs.audit.key.deleted
tier: essential
gating: DELETE overlaps the alarm mask cached on the key handle
cardinality: once per delete through a handle whose mask covers it

The record that a registry key was deleted from a layer through its own
handle, or that an attempt failed. The key named is the one deleted, the
handle's key, by GUID and full path.

Deleting a layer's metadata key, `Machine\System\Registry\Layers\<Name>`,
deletes the layer itself; the record then also names the layer.

include: caller
field: object.kind              required
  Always `key`.
field: object.key.guid          required
field: object.key.path          required
field: object.key.layer.name    optional
  The layer whose entry was deleted.
field: object.layer.name        optional
  Present when the delete removed a layer, naming it.
field: access.requested         required
  Always `DELETE`.
field: access.granted           required
field: access.matched           required
field: access.audit-mask        required
field: transaction.id           optional
field: request.timed-out        when outcome.success == false
field: outcome.success          required
field: outcome.errno            when outcome.success == false

--- event lcs.audit.key.hidden
tier: essential
gating: DELETE overlaps the alarm mask cached on the key handle
cardinality: once per hide through a handle whose mask covers it

The record that a registry key was hidden in a layer through its own
handle, or that an attempt failed. Hiding writes a marker that masks the
key in every lower layer without deleting it there, so to every reader the
key is gone; it is gated on `DELETE`, as deleting is.

include: caller
field: object.kind              required
  Always `key`.
field: object.key.guid          required
field: object.key.path          required
field: object.key.layer.name    optional
  The layer the marker was written in.
field: access.requested         required
  Always `DELETE`.
field: access.granted           required
field: access.matched           required
field: access.audit-mask        required
field: mutation.sequence        optional
field: transaction.id           optional
field: request.timed-out        when outcome.success == false
field: outcome.success          required
field: outcome.errno            when outcome.success == false

--- event lcs.audit.key.created
tier: essential
gating: a matching SACL audit ACE on the parent key, for KEY_CREATE_SUB_KEY
cardinality: once per key created

The record that a registry key was created. Creating a key checks only
`KEY_CREATE_SUB_KEY` on its parent, so the parent's SACL decides, by the
rule `lcs.audit.key.opened` applies to an open: a success audit ACE on the
parent that matches the caller. No handle exists yet to carry an alarm
mask.

Only a key that was made is recorded. A create that found the key already
there and opened it instead writes nothing here; it is an open, and
`lcs.audit.key.opened` records it if the key's own SACL asks. The record
is written once the key exists, so a create whose handle then fails to
reach the caller is still recorded, because the key was still made. A
transacted create is recorded when staged.

include: caller
field: object.kind              required
  Always `key`.
field: object.key.guid          required
  The new key.
field: object.key.path          required
  The new key's full path.
field: object.key.layer.name    optional
  The layer the key was created in.
field: object.key.created       required
  Always true.
field: object.key.volatile      required
field: object.key.volatile-requested  required
  Equal to `object.key.volatile`, since only a key that was made is
  recorded.
field: object.key.symlink       required
field: object.sd.length         required
  The new key's descriptor, as built from the parent's inheritable ACEs
  and the caller's token.
field: object.sd.owner          optional
field: access.requested         required
  Always `KEY_CREATE_SUB_KEY`, the right checked on the parent.
field: access.granted           required
  What the parent check granted.
field: transaction.id           optional
field: outcome.success          required
  Always true.

--- event lcs.audit.key.descriptor.changed
tier: essential
gating: always when the change includes the SACL; otherwise WRITE_DAC or WRITE_OWNER overlaps the alarm mask cached on the key handle
cardinality: once per descriptor change it covers

The record that a registry key's security descriptor was changed through a
key handle, or that an attempt failed.

**A change to the SACL is always recorded**, whatever the handle's mask.
It takes `ACCESS_SYSTEM_SECURITY`, which only `SeSecurityPrivilege` grants,
such changes are rare, and removing a SACL is exactly how someone hides
what follows. It is recorded once the handle's own access check admits it:
a caller whose handle lacks `ACCESS_SYSTEM_SECURITY` is refused before any
record, so asking for a SACL change cannot be used to write records.
Owner, group and DACL changes follow the handle's mask, as writes do.

Both descriptors are recorded by length, SHA-256 digest and owner, never
in full. Whether the SACL was part of the change is
`object.sd.components`.

include: caller
field: object.kind              required
  Always `key`.
field: object.key.guid          required
field: object.key.path          required
field: object.sd.components     required
  The parts the caller asked to change, its `security_info`.
field: object.sd.length         optional
  The descriptor as written: the merge of the caller's parts into the
  old one. Absent when the request failed before the merge.
field: object.sd.length-previous  optional
  Absent when the request failed before the old descriptor was read.
field: object.sd.digest         optional
  Present with `object.sd.length`.
field: object.sd.digest-previous  optional
  Present with `object.sd.length-previous`.
field: object.sd.owner          optional
  Present with `object.sd.length`.
field: object.sd.owner-previous   optional
  Present with `object.sd.length-previous`.
field: access.requested         required
  The rights the change needs: `WRITE_OWNER` for the owner or group,
  `WRITE_DAC` for the DACL, `ACCESS_SYSTEM_SECURITY` for the SACL.
field: access.granted           required
field: access.matched           required
  Zero when the record exists only because the change included the SACL.
field: access.audit-mask        required
field: transaction.id           optional
field: request.timed-out        when outcome.success == false
  True when the source did not answer in time, so the change may have
  been applied after all.
field: outcome.success          required
field: outcome.errno            when outcome.success == false

--- event lcs.audit.transaction.committed
tier: essential
gating: the transaction staged at least one recorded write
cardinality: once per transaction that staged a recorded write

The record of how a registry transaction ended, written for a transaction
that staged at least one write some other record covers. Transacted
writes are recorded when they are staged; this says whether they took
effect, joined to them on `transaction.id`.

It is written once, at the end: when `REG_IOC_COMMIT` leaves the
transaction committed or otherwise finished, when it times out, or when
its fd is closed without a commit. A commit that fails and leaves the
transaction open writes nothing yet.

**A commit that timed out may have taken effect.** The commit was sent
and not answered; `transaction.commit-outstanding` true says so, and the
source may have applied it before the reply was lost.

**The subject depends on how it ended.** On `REG_IOC_COMMIT` it is the
subject who committed, not who staged the writes. A transaction that ended
by timeout or by its fd closing has nobody acting, and names the subject
that staged its first recorded write.

include: caller
field: transaction.id           required
field: transaction.state        required
  The state it ended in: `committed` on success.
field: transaction.commit-outstanding  when outcome.success == false
field: outcome.success          required
  True only when the transaction committed.
field: outcome.errno            optional
  Present when a `REG_IOC_COMMIT` call failed; absent for a transaction
  that ended without one.
field: outcome.reason           when outcome.success == false
  values: aborted | timed-out | source-error
  closed: false
  `aborted` when its fd was closed without a commit or a layer it wrote
  was deleted, `timed-out` when it passed its deadline, `source-error`
  when its source failed or went away.

--- event lcs.audit.backup.started
tier: essential
gating: none — audited unconditionally

The record that a registry subtree backup began, emitted **before any data
is read**. Backup is a privilege-gated bulk operation that bypasses
per-key access checks entirely, so this trail is the only evidence it
happened at all — which is why it is audited whatever the target key's
SACL says.

The start record exists so that an operation dying partway still leaves
evidence that it began. A start with no matching end is itself the
finding.

Emission failure fails the operation with `EIO`, unlike every other LCS
event: an unrecorded bulk read of the configuration store is not permitted
to proceed.

include: caller
field: object.key.guid          required
field: operation.fd             required
  The descriptor the backup stream was written to.

--- event lcs.audit.backup.ended
tier: essential
gating: none — audited unconditionally

The record that a registry subtree backup finished, successfully or
otherwise. Pairs with `lcs.audit.backup.started` on the same key.

Emission failure here does not affect the result, which has already been
determined by the time this is written.

include: caller
field: object.key.guid          required
field: outcome.success          required
field: outcome.errno            when outcome.success == false

--- event lcs.audit.restore.started
tier: essential
gating: none — audited unconditionally

The record that a registry subtree restore began, emitted **before any
state is modified**. As with backup, this is a privilege-gated bulk
operation that bypasses per-key access checks, and is audited regardless of
the target's SACL.

A restore rewrites configuration wholesale, so a start with no matching
end means the store may be in a partially rewritten state.

Emission failure fails the operation with `EIO`.

include: caller
field: object.key.guid          required
field: operation.fd             required
  The descriptor the restore stream was read from.

--- event lcs.audit.restore.ended
tier: essential
gating: none — audited unconditionally

The record that a registry subtree restore finished, successfully or
otherwise. Pairs with `lcs.audit.restore.started` on the same key.

include: caller
field: object.key.guid          required
field: outcome.success          required
field: outcome.errno            when outcome.success == false

--- event lcs.source.response.rejected
tier: essential
gating: none — every rejection is recorded

The record that LCS rejected data a registry source sent it. Sources are
userspace backends serving configuration into the kernel, so this is the
boundary where the kernel refuses to believe what userspace told it.

**This is the one LCS event with no caller.** It describes a source
misbehaving rather than a principal acting, and there is frequently no
principal involved — the data arrived on a source channel, not through a
syscall.

The failure classes distinguish malformed structure from semantic
impossibility: a security descriptor that will not parse is a different
problem from a sequence number from the future or two layers claiming the
same winning sequence.

field: source.rsi.slot          required
field: source.rsi.hive          optional
field: request.id               optional
field: request.op-code          optional
field: object.key.guid          optional
  Present when the rejected data could be attributed to a specific key.
field: outcome.reason           required
  values: malformed-security-descriptor | malformed-layer-name | unknown-rsi-status-code | future-sequence-number | duplicate-winning-sequence-tie | malformed-layer-metadata-security-descriptor | malformed-key-name | malformed-value-name | malformed-response-payload | malformed-key-metadata | malformed-value-payload | malformed-delete-layer-orphan-list
  closed: false
  The source-validation failure class.

--- event lcs.config.value.rejected
tier: standard
gating: none — every rejected configuration value is recorded
cardinality: once per rejected value

The record that LCS read one of its own configuration values, rejected it,
and carried on with what it had. Identical in shape and meaning to
`kmes.config.value.rejected`, differing only in which key the values live
under.

Validation is reject-or-keep and never clamps, so this event is the only
place where what an administrator wrote and what LCS is running on can be
seen to differ.

Emission failure preserves the retained configuration — a failure to record
the rejection must not change which value is in force.

field: config.key.path          required
field: config.name              required
field: config.expected.type     required
field: config.expected.min      required
field: config.expected.max      required
field: config.received.kind     required
field: config.received.type     when config.received.kind == wrong-type
field: config.received.value    when config.received.kind == out-of-range
field: config.value             required
  The value retained.
